 \begin{abstract}
-Choosing effective interventions in complex systems often requires costly experiments. Causal Bayesian optimization guides these experiments using a causal graph, but errors in that graph can lead to poor predictions or exclude the best interventions. In practice, relationships between groups of variables may be more reliable than the detailed links between individual variables. Here we introduce causal Bayesian optimizers that use this coarse knowledge and intervene jointly on variables within selected groups. Their decisions are insensitive to graph errors that leave the relationships between groups unchanged. However, requiring joint interventions can also limit the outcomes they can achieve. We characterize this loss and identify conditions under which grouping variables preserves the best achievable outcome. Controlled experiments and benchmarks across three optimizer families examine these benefits and limitations. Our results show how coarse causal knowledge can support intervention optimization while making explicit the tradeoff between robustness to graph errors and flexibility in choosing interventions.
+Choosing effective interventions in complex systems often requires costly experiments. Causal Bayesian optimization guides these experiments using a causal graph, but errors in that graph can lead to poor predictions or exclude the best interventions. In practice, relationships between groups of variables may be more reliable than the detailed links between individual variables. Here we introduce causal Bayesian optimizers that use this coarse knowledge and intervene jointly on variables within selected groups. Their decisions are insensitive to graph errors that leave the relationships between groups unchanged. However, requiring joint interventions can also limit the outcomes they can achieve. We characterize this loss and identify conditions under which grouping variables preserves the best achievable outcome. Controlled experiments across three optimizer families examine these benefits and limitations, separating action restrictions from finite-budget differences due to modeling and optimization.
 \end{abstract}
 
